(======================================================================)
(  VOX // CONWAY'S GAME OF LIFE  --  written as English sentences,     )
(======================================================================)

a number called WIDTH is 40.
a number called HEIGHT is 20.
a number called GENERATIONS is 14.

a buffer called grid is 800 bytes in size.
a buffer called 'next grid' is 800 bytes in size.

(Step off one edge of the board and come back on at the opposite edge)
To wrap with a number called position and a number called step and a number called 'board size'.
  Return a number, {{{position subtract 1} add step add 'board size'} modulo 'board size'} add 1.

To 'cell index' with a number called row and a number called column.
  Return a number, {{row subtract 1} multiply WIDTH} add column.

To 'alive neighbors' with a number called row and a number called column.
  a number called tally is 0.
  a number called 'neighbor row' is 0.
  a number called 'neighbor column' is 0.
  a number called state is 0.
  a number called 'neighbor index' is 0.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and -1 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and -1 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and -1 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and 0 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and -1 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and 1 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and 0 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and -1 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and 0 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and 1 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and 1 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and -1 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and 1 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and 0 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  the 'neighbor row' is wrap of row and 1 and HEIGHT.
  the 'neighbor column' is wrap of column and 1 and WIDTH.
  the 'neighbor index' is 'cell index' of 'neighbor row' and 'neighbor column'.
  the state is byte 'neighbor index' of grid.
  If state is equal to 1 then, the tally is tally add 1.
  Return a number, tally.

(print row is single-argument (row) -- a loop-expansion candidate.)
(Each cell is appended into a line buffer, then the whole row is printed)
(as a single line. Building one buffer and printing it once lays the row)
(out as a line in one step.)

To 'print row' with a number called row.
  a buffer called line is 64 bytes in size.
  For each column from 1 to WIDTH,
      a number called index is 'cell index' of row and column,
      a number called state is byte index of grid,
      a text called mark is ".",
      If state is equal to 1 then, the mark is "#".
      Append mark to line.
  Print line.

(step cell takes a row and a column, because no cell's fate depends on)
(any other cell in its row. One chained expansion then walks the whole)
(grid: the row clause is the outer loop, the column clause the inner.)

To 'step cell' with a number called row and a number called column.
  a number called index is 'cell index' of row and column.
  a number called neighbors is 'alive neighbors' of row and column.
  a number called state is byte index of grid.
  a number called 'next state' is 0.
  If state is equal to 1 and neighbors is equal to 2 then, the 'next state' is 1.
  If state is equal to 1 and neighbors is equal to 3 then, the 'next state' is 1.
  If state is equal to 0 and neighbors is equal to 3 then, the 'next state' is 1.
  Set byte index of 'next grid' to 'next state'.

(run generation is single-argument (generation) -- folds header/render/step/copy)
(into one homemade function, which is what lets the entire simulation)
(drive loop collapse into a single loop-expansion statement below.)

To 'run generation' with a number called generation.
  Print "-- Generation {generation} of {GENERATIONS} --".
  'print row' of each row from 1 to HEIGHT.
  'step cell' of each row from 1 to HEIGHT and each column from 1 to WIDTH.
  copy 'next grid' to grid.

a number called 'seed index' is 0.

(--- Seed: an R-pentomino (chaotic for ~1100 gens in an infinite grid) ---)
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 9 and 20.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 9 and 21.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 10 and 19.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 10 and 20.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 11 and 20.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.

(--- Seed: a glider drifting diagonally in the corner ---)
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 2 and 3.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 3 and 4.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 4 and 2.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 4 and 3.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.
the 'seed index' is 'cell index' of 4 and 4.
Set byte 'seed index' of grid to 1.

Print "========================================".
Print "  VOX -- Conway's Game of Life".
Print "  40x20 toroidal grid, R-pentomino + glider".
Print "========================================".

Create a timer called clock.
Start the clock.

(The entire simulation drive loop, collapsed to one line: loop expansion)
(calling a homemade function once per generation, no manual counter.)

'run generation' of each generation from 1 to GENERATIONS.

Stop the clock.
Print "========================================".
Print "Ran {GENERATIONS} generations on an 800-cell grid in:".
Print the clock's duration in milliseconds.
Print "milliseconds -- native assembly, no VM, no GC, no interpreter.".
